Z||000000||DaveL||06-06-2001||03:48 PM||||OK, 87 2.5, lots of knocking or ticking depending on who's listening. I found a crack in the exhaust manifold. The mechanic says he thinks it sounds like a wrist pin rattling in addition to the leak. He advises not to fix it until I have to. Runs great but the noise is embarrassing as hell.<P>Now I'm left wondering just how much quieter it'd get if I just fix the manifold first.<P>What the heck are "wrist pins" ? Just the name sounds expensive....<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||rockcrawl||06-06-2001||04:16 PM||||The wrist pins hold the pistons onto the connecting rods. It is possible that one of them has partially seized, and during each revolution of the crank the piston skirt is slapping the cylinder wall. Possibly due to the engine being overheated or run too hot. It makes a sound somewhere between a knock and a tap.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Leadfoot||06-06-2001||04:23 PM||||A wirst pin connects the piston to the connecting rod.  I don't think that is your problem though.  I had an '86 s/10 blazer with the Iron Duke in it (2.5L).  The thing was durable as all hell but it had an annoying knock to it.  I drove it like that for 30k with no problems until one day the radiator gave out on me an caused my engine to overheat.  Yeah! that stupid temperature dummy light never came on.  Anyways, I tore apart the engine and found that the #1 cylinder had a nasty ridge on it.  Which means it experienced the most wear.  I took the block to my local machinist and he said the knocking was attributed to piston slap.  Made sense at the time.  Either way, looks like you need a rebuild to get rid of that knock.  By the way, my timing gear went out on me around 80K which was about the same time I notice the knocking.  Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000003||cadaver||06-06-2001||06:05 PM||||I dont know about the knock without hearding it. So I wont tell you its this or its that. But do get the crack welded. That will make a bit of noise itself.||||reg||1
